    "pageInfo": "100598",
    "abstractText": "<h4>Background</h4>The global antimicrobial resistance (AMR) crisis necessitates innovative therapeutic approaches beyond traditional antibiotics. Cold atmospheric plasma (CAP), an ionized gas containing reactive species, has emerged as a potent physical antimicrobial technology with broad-spectrum activity. This systematic review critically evaluates current evidence on CAP's antimicrobial mechanisms, efficacy against diverse pathogens, and clinical translation potential.<h4>Methods</h4>We conducted a systematic literature search following Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses guidelines across PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science databases (January 2018-December 2025). Included studies investigated CAP's antimicrobial properties <i>in vitro</i>, <i>in vivo</i>, or clinically.<h4>Results</h4>Analysis of 64 included studies revealed that CAP's antimicrobial effects primarily stem from reactive oxygen and nitrogen species generation, causing oxidative damage to microbial membranes, proteins, and DNA. CAP demonstrates significant efficacy against multi-drug-resistant bacteria, viruses, fungi, and biofilms. Clinical studies show particular promise in chronic wound management, with randomized controlled trials demonstrating accelerated healing and reduced microbial load.<h4>Conclusion</h4>CAP represents a promising multi-target approach to antimicrobial therapy, offering a multi-target mechanism that circumvents conventional resistance pathways. While standardization and dosimetry challenges remain, CAP holds immense potential for integration into clinical practice against AMR.",
